 For eight weeks in 2013, Broadchurch Season 1 gripped UK viewers' attention like nothing before. Seasons 2 and 3 were just as enthralling, and even nine years after the show's end, Broadchurch ​​​​​​has continued to be a hugely popular title all over the world. Broadchurch is another level of perfection; you won't find many people who disagree, but all these years later, there's an unanswered question that I'm still thinking about.
 Joe Admitted He Was "In Love" With Danny in 'Broadchurch's Season 1 Finale
 Close 
 After the Broadchurch Season 1 finale showed us the events that occurred on the night of Danny Latimer's death, we saw Alec questioning him. When asked about the nature of his relationship with Danny, Joe immediately replied: "I was in love with him." This was a shocking moment in the hit British crime drama ; their relationship seemed inappropriate in the flashback, but an admission of love was a whole other level of unexpected. 
 Things between them didn't seem to progress past hugging, and Joe was adamant that he'd never touched the boy in an inappropriate way , claiming that he only ever asked Danny "to hold [him]." At one point in the interview, Joe told Alec he couldn't understand why he felt that way towards Danny, and it would seem that later installments of Broadchurch were unsure about it, too.
 Over 13 years after the Broadchurch Season 1 finale, some viewers are still confused about Joe's feelings for Danny. Some believe Joe had more of a fatherly love for the boy, backed up by the fact that their secret meetups only began because Mark hit Danny, which led him to seek comfort in his best friend's father. However, saying he was "in love" with a child didn't exactly scream parental role, either.
 It was a massively unexpected revelation for everyone involved , but in Seasons 2 and 3, this astounding detail seemed to vanish into thin air. Even his wife, Ellie, questioned whether this made Joe a pedophile, so why didn't the thrilling mystery series shed some more light on this matter in its later seasons?
 'Broadchurch's Forgotten Joe Detail Could've Made His Acquittal Even More Impactful
 After Broadchurch Season 1, Episode 8, the incredible crime thriller series seemingly avoided bringing this up again. The main focus was on Joe killing Danny , but not so much on the reason behind his meetings with the child in the first place.
 Of course, Joe pleaded not guilty during his trial in Season 2, and Ellie's attack on him in the Broadchurch Season 1 finale led to his confession to Alec being thrown out. Still, while this technically couldn't be used against him in court, it was baffling that his feelings for Danny weren't referenced during the trial , or that the residents of Broadchurch didn't mention it more afterward.
 Still, there was evidence that could've been used against him, in theory. Joe admitting he gave Danny a phone and cash that was supposed to be used for his family's trip to Florida, all because he "wanted [Danny] to love him," couldn't technically be used as evidence. However, the fact that Joe purchased the phone, which was later tracked back to him and found in his possession, should've really been something to work off of.
 Broadchurch is considered a masterpiece crime series for a lot of reasons, but especially because the events of Season 1 – the horrific murder that rocked the whole town – played a part in subsequent installments of the show. Danny's death ensured life in the titular town would never be the same again , and the show did a great job of demonstrating that. Yet, it failed to expand upon one of the most important details of the entire mystery.
 After Joe was banished from the coastal town in Season 2, he changed his name, but Mark still managed to track him down in Liverpool in Season 3. Their conversation was one of the most powerful in the entire show , but once again, no real mention of Joe being in love with Danny.
 Mark demanded that Joe talk him through the events of that awful night for the sake of closure, but the murderer instead spoke about how his actions impacted him ; he didn't talk about the motivation behind the killing on a deeper level. The only thing Joe said that could be considered as such was when he explained that he'd come to their house and had a "moment," but that wasn't really enough to argue he'd acknowledged his feelings for Danny.
 While it's understandable that perhaps Broadchurch didn't want to focus on this detail too much , at least not on-screen, there were so many opportunities for the show to at least make a subtle nod to Joe being in love with Danny: Ellie arguing with Tom about defending his father (one of Olivia Colman's best performances to date), Joe being banished from the town, or even Mark discovering Joe was living elsewhere using a new name.
 In fact, Joe's name change would've been the perfect chance for Broadchurch to remind us of this , as he could've wanted to hide his identity from local parents in Liverpool, or been put on a register despite being acquitted. Unfortunately, the show somewhat moved on from this integral part of Joe's story.
 Over 13 years after Joe's admission, and over nine since the show ended, I'm sure I'm not the only one who is still wondering: Why did Broadchurch forget Joe was in love with Danny? Many of us would be curious to find out whether this was a storytelling choice, or perhaps a result of production restrictions that led to scenes being cut, which would've clarified more. All these years later, it's unlikely we'll ever find out, but seeing as so many of us still love the show that brought us David Tennant and Olivia Colman's iconic detective duo , it's worth questioning.
